WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.240
- I got an email from an aquarium,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.410
and it changed my mind about aquariums.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.910
Because biology and zoology...
never really interested me all that much.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.890
For me, somewhere like this,
The Deep in the city of Hull,

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:17.730
wasn't somewhere I'd visit because, yes,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.670
important for conservation,
vital for science,

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.200
but... it's just some fish, right?

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.630
But then they sent me an email
and asked me if I'd be interested

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.720
in looking at their life-support system.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.880
Now, I've only ever heard
the words "life-support system"

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.520
in the context of spaceflight,
either realistic,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.460
like the oxygen scrubbers
and water recyclers

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.980
on the International Space Station,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.730
or in... less realistic situations.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:40.300
- Life-support systems
sustaining on emergency.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.010
- But The Deep, here?

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:44.700
It has a life-support system.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.980
- We are a closed system.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.340
So, much like your spaceship,
we are isolated.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.120
There's no input or output
to the oceans whatsoever.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.610
The water inside that system or exhibit
is constantly recirculating

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.960
between the exhibit and
the life-support system.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.980
So, everything that we have here,
we have to control,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.420
including making our own seawater.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.280
The water starts off, just as it would
in your home, out of the tap.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:07.910
Now, the water is very, very
hard around here.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.240
It's lots of calcium
carbonate in the water,

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.010
so the first thing we
have to do is soften it,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:15.880
so that is the removal of that calcium
and magnesium ions from the water.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.200
So, these are full of an ionic resin,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.890
and they scavenge those
ions out of the water.

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:22.390
We don't want calcium to build up

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.760
in our life-support systems,
on our heating elements.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.580
So, what we're left with, in this
great container over here

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.090
is roughly 30,000 litres
of softened water

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.840
that has gone through this
reverse osmosis machine.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.740
It's basically pumping water
across a very fine membrane

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.260
under very high pressure to
give us very, very pure water.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:46.140
To that pure water we then
add artificial sea salt.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.963
We then pump it around where
needed around the building.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.020
So, this is a really good example
of a small-scale life-support system

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.730
that we would use for some
small stand-alone tanks,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.910
like this research system here.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.795
Now, this has got all the components
of a life-support system

00:02:01.795 --> 00:02:05.370
that you would normally find
in an aquarium like this,

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.220
and this is all scaled up
on a huge scale for things

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:11.870
like our large
2.5-million-litre system.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.860
What we have at the back here,
that's a foam fractionator.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:18.330
So, all of these micro bubbles
that are being injected

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.790
at the base of this foam fractionator
are scavenging organic molecules.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.260
Now, these molecules and compounds
are actually hydrophobic.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.800
They don't like water.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.560
And what they want to do
is they want to adhere

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.670
to the surface of this micro bubble.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.840
And they rise to the surface,

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.630
and we then remove them
via the cone at the top.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.260
This is a bio tower.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.040
So, fish excrete ammonia and urea.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.700
Now, we have to convert
those to less toxic forms.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.920
Inside here, we have a lot of bio-balls,
and that is a huge, huge surface area.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.380
The bacteria that live on these bio-balls

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:53.743
are converting it through
to nitrite, then to nitrate,

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:58.910
and we can then complete that process
by reducing that to nitrogen gas,

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:00.960
which can just off-gas
into the atmosphere.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.240
We've got the sand filtration here.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.330
So, water enters the top of this unit.
It goes through a bed of sand,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.310
which scavengers out all those
particulates in the water,

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.340
and then exits the bottom clean.

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:18.160
One of the last steps in the
life-support process is ultraviolet light.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.890
It breaks down cell membranes.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:23.570
So, we're really looking at
anti-microbial action within these units.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.640
So, water is constantly recirculating

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:29.970
between the life-support
system and the exhibit.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.120
- As I talked to the team
here, I realised that,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.860
sure, you can call this an aquarium,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.840
but you could also call
it "a spaceship for sharks".

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.940
You've got creatures contained
in a higher pressure environment.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.270
And okay, it's not the vacuum
of space out here, sure.

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:48.460
But if these walls are somehow breached,
the environment inside will spray out.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.260
And if it's not fixed quickly,
those fish are dead.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.840
And if that environment isn't controlled 
and maintained constantly and carefully,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.331
they're also dead.

00:03:58.331 --> 00:04:02.031
And that takes a lot more equipment
and a lot more precision

00:04:02.031 --> 00:04:03.383
than you might think.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:06.870
- So, just as we have a
small foam fractionator

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.990
on our smaller life-support systems,

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:12.630
this is the scale of this skimmer
on our large system.

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.170
Now, this is part of the
building, it's so huge.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.510
Now, a key part of maintaining
this is making sure

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.840
the mixture of gas is correct
that we're pumping into it.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:22.760
So, here we have a gas panel.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.800
Now, this is where we alter
the mixture of air and ozone 

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.670
that is going into
the super-skimmer.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.740
So, that's helping to break down
those organic particles, molecules,

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:37.120
so that the skimming can be
that much more efficient.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.930
So, we're going down into the
heart of the building now,

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:41.760
into the basement, and
that's where the really large

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.770
life-support equipment is
located, some of which is so large

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:50.980
it forms the fabric of the building itself
and the pipework is built into the ground.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.860
So, we're now three floors down,

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.260
and we're only just at the
base of the super-skimmer.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.350
We saw the cone right at the top.
Now, we're right down at the base,

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:02.620
so it just gives you a sense of scale
for how big that super-skimmer actually is,

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.060
and how much a part of the building
it actually is.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.920
The water then comes back
out of the super-skimmer

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:11.370
and enters our giant sand filters.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.610
We need to remove all the
particulates out of the water,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.770
those little pieces that you
might see floating around,

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.330
bits of food, things like that.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.810
So, here we have some
very large banks of UV.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:26.340
So, water is constantly passing
and exposing microbes to that UV light.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.410
And then the last step in the process

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.520
is the huge pumps over there
take the water

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.600
and pump it back into the main exhibit

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:36.240
and we start the whole process
over and over again.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:37.820
It happens constantly,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:43.140
and the water is going round and round
completely about once every three hours.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.150
So, here we are above our
main exhibit, Endless Oceans.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:48.110
Now, this is a tropical marine system.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.910
It's 2.5 million litres.
It's 10 metres deep.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:54.350
And in here is where we keep
all of our really key species,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.840
including some that are unfortunately
now critically endangered in the wild.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.360
We do call it a life-support system

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.150
because it is doing exactly that:
it is sustaining life.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.620
We're controlling every
single water parameter

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:08.900
in order that the species that
we're keeping here are thriving.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.970
We are responsible for every
single life at The Deep.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:14.853
- Thank you so much to all
the team at The Deep in Hull.

00:06:14.853 --> 00:06:16.800
There's a link to them
down in the description.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.800
And thank you to my dive cameraman, Tom!

